				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/grigori-karpin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4558017); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=4558017&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605818723" alt="Grigori Karpin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=4558017)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/grigori-karpin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4558017); return false;" >Grigori Karpin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1604897572 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">09 Nov 2020 04:52</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4814345">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">►&nbsp;my&nbsp;detailed&nbsp;thoughts◄</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>A single car wove through the vast green forest, it's green exterior blending in with the earthly surroundings.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>So, I won’t mention this a lot but when the “its” is possessive of an thing, then it is “its” not “it’s.” This happens a few times so you’ll want to make global changes.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>it's breaks to slow but you didn't care too much</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>If it is referring to a vehicle, it is “brakes.”</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>You didn't say anything. And never did, which was a decision you would come to regret years later, when Kondraki was in a casket when it was too late for you to say Lay off the drink, Konny and just maybe he would listen if you had actually said that before he destroyed the very site they were in. But that is another story for another time.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>This hurts the flow of the narrative, it is an aside, but one that doesn’t go anywhere or further develop the narrative. I would either cut it or trim it down dramatically.</em> I would suggest any tangential paragraphs be cut unless they build up the narrative as a whole. If its &quot;another story for another time&quot; don't mention it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>You begin laughing hysterically, delving into the point of insanity. You were still laughing when the guards carried him out of the room and injected you with sedatives.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>Who is “him” in this sentence? This was a conversation between Gears and the EC representative, who had just stepped outta the room.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>When you reach the bottom floor- you are a short boy and your legs simply cannot take you down steps fast enough -you find no one there, not even your mom. You look around in anguish, and by the time dawn reaches you pick up the phone and dial 911. <strong>You realize later, when you are an accomplished Level 4 researcher, that his father had left to join the Foundation, promoted to O5-1. You never forgave him for that, even decades later.</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>The bold sentence really confuses me. Who is person who went to join the foundation and became an O5? Was it Gears’ grandfather? If so, I would clearly state that instead of the round about way you mention it. Also who is Gears not forgiving, his father for committing suicide or the “grandfather” who abandoned his father?</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>You're fourteen and still in the orphanage</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>Why is Gears in the orphanage? His father committed suicide, but just a couple paragraphs ago you mention a mother…</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>It's been two years, and you're back in your battered old green car, driving up the winding road into Site-19</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>“Two years” since what? The jumping around in time means you need to be more specific with your language. Nothing wrong with a flashback but it means you don’t want to leave your reader lost.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>It feels.. good, to make a difference. To be treated the same.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>This comes out of nowhere… what good is Gears doing? He seems to have just been brought back to Site-19 after a lengthy stay at a psychiatric hospital. ALSO, I will note that I feel it unlikely that the foundation would reinstate someone who had a psychotic break into his old security clearance without significant rehabilitation and testing the water. Nothing is really mentioned about his road back to being a researching and that is jarring.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>You've been briefed that you are now the new project head, which is something as a surprise considering this is your first day back and you contained a monumental containment breach two years ago.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>Not only is this a surprise, it’s completely unbelievable. Before he was a junior researcher under Fritz Willie and now he’s a project head? That just wouldn’t happen after 2 years in a hospital. He hadn’t even worked his way up to project head before being hospitalized.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Years later, when your nerve endings has been cauterized and high-ranking members of the Foundation call you a success story, you realize it was your dad who put you on this project, which opens a whole new can of worms. You felt anger and disbelief. This is how his dad repaid him. By making head on a research project instead of picking up the phone and saying Hello.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>His dad has been dead since he was 12! How would this make any sense…. Now if you said it was nepotism from his grandfather, sure. But you sorta go a roundabout way, I would suggest being direct in your language.</em></p>
<hr /></div>
</div>
</div>
<p><strong><span style="text-decoration: underline;">Overall</span></strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>I know in one of the comments above you mention that the runon sentences are an intentional thing, but they are very distracting. It’s hard to keep your attention to all the facts laid out in one sentence when they go on too long.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>There’s a lot of grammatical errors, like extra punctuation or not enough (… always requires three, for example). I would go through this with a fine-toothed comb re: grammar.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>There’s so much cursing, and everyone is very unprofessional in their dialogue. This is especially true of the exchange between Gears and the EC representative. Gears is a Doctor and a professional, why is he acting like this? He’s hostile out of the gate and just cursing up a storm. Really took me out of the scene.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>There’s such a leap from Mary being someone Gears once went on a date with to being in a relationship to being pregnant… its shocking. Relationships shouldn’t just be used as tools for narrative, a reader wants to be able to relate to the feelings of the character. Also, you’ve constantly been saying Gears has no feelings, but then you’re showing his feelings left and right… I would suggest dropping that recurring theme, as it clashes with your actual action in the story.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>What are you going for with this tale? What tone? It comes off bleak but also drags on. It feels like a history of Gears but without much in the way of emotional payoff.</li>
</ul>

							</div>

								
