				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5336470&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605818723" alt="cybersqyd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5336470)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;" >cybersqyd</a></span> <span class="odate time_1605723657 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Nov 2020 18:20</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4821782">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>This feels longer than it needs to be and shorter than it needs to be simultaneously: if I had to describe it, I would say that the focus is wrong throughout; or perhaps that it lacks focus. It feels like this is meant as a retrospective of the whole of gears life, covering his entire growth as a person and how he went from being meek and timid at the start to a cold machine at the end, except all the bits that actually convey that change and growth have been cut.</p>
<p>Like, we have the introduction of his relationship with his wife here but not the end; so there's no impetus for him to shut off emotions because of that; which means his actual growth from warm and caring to cold is offscreen. Even before this, you have things that feel like they should be big impetuses for change, like the Cheyenne Point visit, that feel like they don't really do much.</p>
<p>Even then, the focus isn't quite directly on this so it just feels&#8230;iffy? It feels like this needs some kinda opening establishing why this story is being told; like, it's kinda cliche but perhaps intermingling the present time stuff with like. an interview at the end of the arc kinda, probing into why Gears is like this now say; so the progression is like. stylised versions of his retelling or w/e.</p>
<p>At the end of the day, I guess my biggest question is really just 'what do you expect a reader to get out of this?'</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><em>Gonna note that I can miss stuff; flag things which are right as being wrong and indeed flag things which are deliberately wrong.</em><br />
<em>I'll likely use <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">strikethrough</span> to indicate stuff to remove and <span style="color: green">green text</span> to indicate stuff to add.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>A single car wove through the vast green forest, its green exterior blending in with the earthly surroundings.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>something about 'its green exterior' feels off to me and I can't quite put my finger on what; I think it might be the relatively formal sounding 'exterior' or possibly the repetition of 'green' but??</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It crunched over rocks, making too much noise for it be so close to a Foundation site. But no one really cared, as no one really lived out here anyway.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The introduction of someone who might care (the foundation site) and then immediate dismissal of there being someone who might hear it feels&#8230;iffy?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It rumbled, it's tires too smooth and its brakes too slow but you didn't care too much.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>this is a really jarring sentence; the way it flows from like, the passive description of stuff happening to the sudden second person is wild, particularly since it's not clear from here where 'you' is in this story yet.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>barely revealing Site-19 with Research Wing 3A being constructed.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>something about the 'with Research Wing 3A being constructed' here feels very iffy; I think it's that it kinda implies &quot;Site-19 contains Research Wing 3A and both are being constructed&quot;, as opposed to &quot;Site-19 is ahead, with a new research wing being constructed&quot;; but I think also it's just a iffy time thing where there's kinda this big broad process at the end following a narrow arrival thing?</p>
<p>Maybe 'where Research Wing 3A is being constructed.' would work better?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It made you feel genuine, as though he really was a top-level researcher attempting to save the world</p>
</blockquote>
<p>pronoun switch mid sentence from 'you' to 'he'</p>
<blockquote>
<p>instead of a<span style="color: green">n</span> ambitious junior-researcher who</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>the sound of your voice, and the smell of alcohol. Was that a Jack Daniels?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'a Jack Daniels' here implies you mean like 'a bottle of jack', whereas if you cut the 'a' it implies 'the smell of jack', which is what you want, I think</p>
<blockquote>
<p>They all had different assignments, but the way Site-19 was laid out, there was one long main corridor before it split off.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This feels like it's a tad overconcise; with it needing just a few more words to set out what is happening; or perhaps:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>They all had different assignments, but because of the way Site-19 was laid out, they had to walk together down the main corridor before they each went their separate ways</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The problem with this is also pronouns: 'they' doesn't include 'you', and you'll want to use 'You all' here instead, assuming you keep up the second person thing.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Kondraki was assigned to Dr. Cann, who headed research for 408. But it was widely known that he let his assistants take control of all of the work</p>
</blockquote>
<p>this implies that Kondraki is one of the assistants, not the person with assistants</p>
<blockquote>
<p>You were assigned to Agent Fritz Willie, and was told he was needed to contain SCP-106.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>the 'was told he was needed' feels really oddly phrased, like, on the one hand, it feels like it's meant to be 'you' but on the other, something like &quot;You were assigned to Agent Fritz Willie to help with the containment of SCP-106&quot; would read better.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>while you was doing paperwork asking for a new desk</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'while you were filling in paperwork to get a new desk' would read better here; 'was' should be 'were' at least though this generally uses present tense throughout so 'are' would be the best.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He vaguely smelled of vodka.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd generally not split the verb/subject like this (i.e., use 'smelled vaguely' over 'vaguely smelled')</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm turning 34, today, Charlie. I've been working here for.. fourteen years. Fresh outta the Marines.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>20 feels young to leave the marines; it's only a couple of years of service depending on when he signed up.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&quot;Don't be a fucking pussy, now.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The now feels weird here; like, it's not quite a command to have a drink now but it's also not quite flowing from the previous thing; and I'd probably cut it</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&quot;Commencing interview. Interviewer, Rachel Adams.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd probably name the interviewer before now; this introduction kinda makes it feel like how she looks is more important than who she is and it feels iffy</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I said, no. There were no fucking flaws in my plan. The stupid fucking D-Class decided to insert the needle into the skip when the Anchors were running low on power, and then that woke up the bastard and-</p>
</blockquote>
<p>There's no indication of how much time passed between the last chapter and now but this feels like a huge character shift: where Gears was kinda, timid and new in the previous section, he's now rude and aggressive; and it doesn't help that we're given like&#8230;no leading context to establish how he feels before this</p>
<blockquote>
<p>with the bureaucratic nonsense that just impedes people and blames doctors for things they didn't see coming</p>
</blockquote>
<p>this is the kinda statement that feels jarring compared to the opening; where we see two different people drinking on the job and a general laissez-faire feeling.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I will not listen to you whine because you decided to fuck up on a containment procedures plan.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'decided' feels weird here; like Gears chose to do a bad job deliberately over like, making a mistake or w/e. It also feels weird here cos the implication of the first section is that Gears had overdone the containment?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>May I remind you I am here from the Ethics Committee</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This feels altogether too late into this interview for this to be brought up now</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and proposing termination.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It feels&#8230;kinda inappropriate for someone to be like &quot;I am going to propose we kill you&quot; to someone else?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Something you still thought about decades later, when tears didn't touch your face or you managed to smile. You were sent to Cheyenne Point Psychiatric Center</p>
</blockquote>
<p>mentioning 'decades' and then following it up with a place gives the impression that he is going to spend decades there, not like, two years.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and returned to bed.</p>
<p>You slowly walked down</p>
</blockquote>
<p>this feels weird cos the first bit implies he has done it already; whereas you then follow it up with a description of him getting into bed.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>You have been sent back to active research duty and is immediately assigned a project to 914.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'and are immediately assigned to a project on SCP-914' would be clearer and more grammatically correct.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>You note that all the experiments are just sticking things <span style="color: green">found</span> inside researcher's pockets into the machine.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>You've been briefed that you are now the new project head, which is something <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">as</span><span style="color: green">of</span> a surprise considering this is your first day back and you contained a monumental containment breach two years ago.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Should the 'contained' not be 'caused'?</p>
<p>Something about the previous two paragraphs made me feel like a length of time had passed, I think the &quot;It feels.. good, to make a difference.&quot; implied to me that Gears had spent a bunch of time working on this already and so it actually being just after he returns feels odd</p>
<blockquote>
<p>You felt anger and disbelief. This is how his dad repaid him.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>another switch to 'he'</p>
<blockquote>
<p>making <span style="color: green">you</span> head on a research project instead of picking up the phone and saying <em>Hello</em>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'of a research project' feels better to me but your call.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Charles Ogden Gears was</p>
</blockquote>
<p>um i think you've forgotten to finish writing this psych report? It kinda feels weirdly jammed in here at the moment too but??</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It's been five months since you had gotten married, and you've loved every single day.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This feels like a weirdly jarring way to introduce Gears' wife, like&#8230;it skips past 'first meeting' and 'falling in love' and straight to 'married' and it feels&#8230;too much of a jump maybe?</p>
<p>This whole section here feels weirdly positioned; it's very idyllic and there's no real &#8230; build up to it or anything</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&quot;About your, uh, condition. Like- what happened to you? Why- why do you act like a robot?&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>this feels weird because everything up to now implies Charles is like, a really warm friendly character, and so there's no like&#8230;growth in him? He just changes suddenly? If this followed the Cheyenne Point Psychiatric Center trip immediately, it'd make more sense, cos there'd be the implied growth/conditioning there to explain it, but instead there's words of him getting married, being enthusiastic about his job and then&#8230;this?</p>
</div>
